# Portfolio Website Development Rules

## Code Quality & Best Practices

### HTML

- Use semantic HTML5 elements (header, nav, main, section, article, footer)
- Include proper meta tags for SEO and social sharing
- Ensure accessibility with proper ARIA labels and alt text
- Use meaningful class names following BEM methodology
- Validate HTML structure and fix any errors

### CSS

- Use CSS custom properties (variables) for consistent theming
- Follow mobile-first responsive design approach
- Use modern CSS features (Grid, Flexbox, CSS animations)
- Keep specificity low and avoid !important unless absolutely necessary
- Use consistent naming conventions and organize styles logically
- Optimize for performance with efficient selectors

### JavaScript

- Use modern ES6+ features and syntax
- Write clean, readable code with proper comments
- Implement error handling and graceful degradation
- Use semantic variable and function names
- Follow DRY (Don't Repeat Yourself) principles

### Performance

- Optimize images (use appropriate formats, lazy loading)
- Minimize HTTP requests
- Use efficient CSS and JavaScript
- Implement proper caching strategies
- Ensure fast loading times (< 3 seconds)

### Accessibility

- Ensure proper color contrast ratios
- Include keyboard navigation support
- Use semantic HTML structure
- Provide alternative text for images
- Test with screen readers

### Security

- Sanitize any user inputs
- Use HTTPS for all external resources
- Implement proper CSP headers
- Avoid inline scripts when possible

## Git & Version Control

### Conventional Commits

Always use conventional commit format for all commits:

```
<type>[optional scope]: <description>

[optional body]

[optional footer(s)]
```

### Commit Types

- `feat`: A new feature
- `fix`: A bug fix
- `docs`: Documentation only changes
- `style`: Changes that do not affect the meaning of the code
- `refactor`: A code change that neither fixes a bug nor adds a feature
- `perf`: A code change that improves performance
- `test`: Adding missing tests or correcting existing tests
- `chore`: Changes to the build process or auxiliary tools

### Examples

```
feat(hero): add animated gradient background
fix(nav): resolve mobile menu toggle issue
style(portfolio): improve card hover animations
refactor(css): organize styles into logical sections
docs(readme): update installation instructions
```

## File Organization

- Keep HTML structure clean and semantic
- Organize CSS into logical sections (variables, base, components, layout)
- Use meaningful file and folder names
- Keep assets organized in appropriate directories

## Browser Support

- Support modern browsers (Chrome, Firefox, Safari, Edge)
- Use progressive enhancement
- Test across different devices and screen sizes
- Ensure graceful degradation for older browsers

## Content Guidelines

- Write clear, engaging copy
- Use professional photography and graphics
- Ensure all content is current and accurate
- Include clear calls-to-action
- Make contact information easily accessible

## Testing

- Test on multiple devices and browsers
- Validate HTML and CSS
- Check accessibility compliance
- Test loading performance
- Verify all links and forms work correctly
